  "summary": "Recent preprints apply single-cell and long-read sequencing to map transposable element activity in aging tissues, organoids and human neurons.",
  "synthesis": "Across these preprints, the dominant thread is the activity and genomic consequences of transposable elements in somatic tissues. In aging female mouse and human visceral adipose tissue, snRNA-seq shows retrotransposon upregulation in human aging. In the brain, LUSTRE maps L1 retrotransposition sites in about 17,600 adult human neurons to estimate somatic insertion rates. Single-cell long-read sequencing of cortical organoids links locus-resolved, human-specific transposable elements to the timing of the radial glia-to-neuron transition, supported by CRISPRi manipulation. What is new is resolution at the single-cell and locus level in human tissue. A separate study estimates neuronal point mutation rates of about 25.9 per diploid genome per year, similar in mice and humans. The remaining two items, an enhancer reporter platform in iPSC-derived organoids and Sanger verification of a Prnp-Prnd deletion junction, are methodological rather than transposon-focused.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-24"
}
